<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<timedtext format="3">
<head>
<pen id="1" b="1" fc="#FFFFFF" fo="254" bc="#140804" bo="170" et="4" ec="#000000" fs="4" sz="110"/>
<wp id="1" ap="7" ah="50" av="92"/>
<ws id="1" ju="2"/>
</head>
<body>
<p t="2500" d="4640" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">A thousand years ago, at the eastern gate of
Mesopotamia, a Kurdish dynasty rose to power.</s></p>
<p t="7180" d="4200" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The great road from Baghdad to Khorasan
ran straight through their lands.</s></p>
<p t="11420" d="2360" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">For nearly one hundred and thirty
years, they held this gate.</s></p>
<p t="13820" d="2920" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They built palaces, led cavalry armies,</s></p>
<p t="16780" d="3280" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and lived through alliances and
wars with neighboring dynasties.</s></p>
<p t="20100" d="1960" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Their name was the Annazids.</s></p>
<p t="22100" d="1692" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Welcome to Dirok.</s></p>
<p t="23833" d="3987" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">So you never miss the pages of Kurdish
history that others tried to erase,</s></p>
<p t="27860" d="1339" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">remember to subscribe.</s></p>
<p t="40620" d="4040" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">East of the Tigris River, the plain
of Mesopotamia meets the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="44700" d="3000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Right on this threshold
rises the city of Hulwan.</s></p>
<p t="47740" d="2759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Known today as Sarpol-e Zahab, this city was,</s></p>
<p t="50540" d="2867" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">according to medieval geographers,
the last city of the plain of Iraq,</s></p>
<p t="53447" d="2385" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">that is, of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="55873" d="2787" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Alwand stream, rising at
Hulwan, flows into the Diyala River,</s></p>
<p t="58700" d="1759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and from there into the Tigris.</s></p>
<p t="60500" d="3000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This land carries a very ancient memory.</s></p>
<p t="63540" d="2879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Four thousand years ago,</s></p>
<p t="66460" d="2412" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Lullubi king Anubanini had his
victory carved into the rocks of Hulwan.</s></p>
<p t="68913" d="3774" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Later, the Kassites came out of this
region and took the throne of Babylon.</s></p>
<p t="72727" d="4572" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">For thousands of years, the ancestors of the
Kurds lived at this gate between Mesopotamia</s></p>
<p t="77340" d="1259" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="83320" d="3920" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The story of the Annazids
begins around the year 990.</s></p>
<p t="87280" d="3719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Abu al-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz,
of the Kurdish Shadhanjan tribe,</s></p>
<p t="91040" d="2026" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">seized power in the city of Hulwan.</s></p>
<p t="93107" d="3012" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The dynasty took its name
from his father, Annaz.</s></p>
<p t="96160" d="4879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Shadhanjan were a powerful Kurdish community
living between the Mesopotamian plain and</s></p>
<p t="101080" d="879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="102000" d="4719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">With the horsemen of his tribe, Abu
al-Fath turned the balance of the region in</s></p>
<p t="106760" d="2386" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">his favor and ruled Hulwan for twenty years.</s></p>
<p t="109187" d="4513" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">And so the eastern gate of Mesopotamia
passed into the hands of a Kurdish emirate.</s></p>
<p t="118420" d="2199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Hulwan was no ordinary city.</s></p>
<p t="120660" d="3319" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The great road linking Baghdad
to Hamadan and Khorasan passed</s></p>
<p t="124020" d="2079" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">through here and climbed into the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="126140" d="3200" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Caravans laden with silk,
spices, horses and cloth,</s></p>
<p t="129380" d="2040" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">pilgrim convoys from Khorasan,</s></p>
<p t="131460" d="3080" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">envoys and armies all had
to pass through this gate.</s></p>
<p t="134580" d="3667" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Annazids guarded the road, and
in return they collected a toll.</s></p>
<p t="138287" d="2932" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But the dynasty's wealth did
not come from the road alone.</s></p>
<p t="141260" d="3600" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The well-watered lands of the Diyala
plain yielded wheat and barley.</s></p>
<p t="144900" d="3120" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The tribes spent the winter in
the mild plains of Mesopotamia,</s></p>
<p t="148060" d="2680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and the summer in the cool mountain pastures.</s></p>
<p t="150780" d="1680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This cycle of summer and</s></p>
<p t="152500" d="3660" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">winter pastures made the plain and
the mountain one single homeland.</s></p>
<p t="160880" d="3160" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Annazid rule rested on a tribal order.</s></p>
<p t="164080" d="5439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The emir was the head of the
dynasty, but he drew his power from
the horsemen of the Shadhanjan tribe.</s></p>
<p t="169560" d="2960" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The lands were shared among
members of the dynasty.</s></p>
<p t="172560" d="2400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The emir's brother Muhalhil governed Shahrazur,</s></p>
<p t="175000" d="3400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">while another brother,
Surkhab, governed Bandanijin.</s></p>
<p t="178440" d="1840" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">At court, envoys were received,</s></p>
<p t="180320" d="3520" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">taxes were collected, and councils
were held with the tribal chiefs.</s></p>
<p t="183880" d="3400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Important decisions were made with
the approval of the tribal elders.</s></p>
<p t="187320" d="3760" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This order reflected the structure of
a Kurdish tribal tradition that endured</s></p>
<p t="191120" d="1260" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">for centuries.</s></p>
<p t="197100" d="2680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The heart of Annazid power was its cavalry.</s></p>
<p t="199820" d="3240" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Lightly armored Kurdish riders,
armed with lances and bows,</s></p>
<p t="203100" d="1560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">moved</s></p>
<p t="204700" d="2160" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">swiftly across the plains and laid
ambushes in the mountain passes.</s></p>
<p t="206900" d="4879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The dynasty's most precious assets were
the castles built on steep mountain slopes.</s></p>
<p t="211820" d="4560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Castles such as Sirwan were both the place
where the treasury was kept and the family's</s></p>
<p t="216420" d="1320" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">refuge in times of danger.</s></p>
<p t="217780" d="4520" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">An emir defeated on the plain could withdraw to
his mountain castle and gather his strength again.</s></p>
<p t="222340" d="4420" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">That is why, even when they lost the plain,
the Annazids could not easily be destroyed.</s></p>
<p t="231480" d="3600" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Annazids' most powerful
neighbor was also a Kurdish dynasty:</s></p>
<p t="235120" d="3120" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Hasanwayhids, who ruled
Dinawar and its surroundings.</s></p>
<p t="238280" d="2680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The two dynasties were bound by marriage ties.</s></p>
<p t="241000" d="2280" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Yet at the same time, they were fierce rivals.</s></p>
<p t="243320" d="4520" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Again and again, the two Kurdish families
clashed over caravan routes and mountain</s></p>
<p t="247880" d="719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">passes.</s></p>
<p t="248640" d="4000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">When the Hasanwayhid emir Badr
ibn Hasanwayh died in 1014,</s></p>
<p t="252680" d="1879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Hasanwayhid power collapsed.</s></p>
<p t="254600" d="3659" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">A large part of their lands
passed to the Annazids.</s></p>
<p t="262980" d="3772" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The dynasty's golden age
came with Abu al-Shawk Faris.</s></p>
<p t="266793" d="4186" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Bearing the title Husam al-Dawla,
this emir ruled for thirty-five years.</s></p>
<p t="271020" d="3759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Under his rule, Annazid lands
stretched from Hulwan to Kermanshah,</s></p>
<p t="274820" d="4159" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">from Dinawar to Shahrazur,
and from Daquq to Bandanijin.</s></p>
<p t="279020" d="4480" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Most of these lands lay along the
eastern edge of the Mesopotamian plain.</s></p>
<p t="283540" d="3799" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the balance between Baghdad and Hamadan,</s></p>
<p t="287380" d="3100" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Annazid cavalry was a force
everyone had to reckon with.</s></p>
<p t="295200" d="4039" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Annazids struck a skillful
balance between powerful neighbors.</s></p>
<p t="299280" d="2973" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">To the west stood the
Abbasid caliphate in Baghdad.</s></p>
<p t="302293" d="2506" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">To the northwest, the Uqaylids ruled Mosul,</s></p>
<p t="304840" d="3000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and to the southwest, the
Mazyadids ruled at Hilla.</s></p>
<p t="307880" d="5719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">To the east, the Kakuyids of Isfahan
laid claim to Hamadan and Dinawar.</s></p>
<p t="313640" d="5040" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Abu al-Shawk sometimes allied with these
neighbors, and sometimes fought them.</s></p>
<p t="318720" d="4799" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Envoys, gifts and marriage ties were
weapons as important as the sword.</s></p>
<p t="323560" d="4359" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Farther away, the Kurdish dynasties of the
Marwanids and the Shaddadids were also on the rise</s></p>
<p t="327960" d="1300" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">in the same age.</s></p>
<p t="333980" d="2959" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But a wound from within weakened the dynasty.</s></p>
<p t="336980" d="3319" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Struggles for the throne broke
out between brothers and nephews.</s></p>
<p t="340340" d="4600" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The rivalry between Abu al-Shawk and his
brother Muhalhil split the tribes in two.</s></p>
<p t="344980" d="4919" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Later, Abu al-Shawk's son Sadi sought
outside help against his uncle Muhalhil.</s></p>
<p t="349940" d="3780" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This division opened the way for
a new storm coming from the east.</s></p>
<p t="358440" d="4879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the middle of the eleventh century, the
Seljuks swiftly conquered the Iranian plateau.</s></p>
<p t="363360" d="3959" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In 1046, the Seljuk commander
Ibrahim Inal marched on Hulwan.</s></p>
<p t="367360" d="1959" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The city was besieged, and it fell.</s></p>
<p t="369360" d="4359" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the same year, Abu al-Shawk died
in the mountain fortress of Sirwan.</s></p>
<p t="373760" d="3399" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Annazids lost their cities
on the Mesopotamian plain,</s></p>
<p t="377200" d="2100" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">but they did not give up the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="384020" d="4492" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The dynasty survived for seventy more
years in its castles and in the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="388553" d="6146" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Muhalhil and the emirs descended from him
ruled around Shahrazur, Daquq and Sirwan.</s></p>
<p t="394740" d="4479" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Around the year 1117, the Annazid
dynasty left the stage of history.</s></p>
<p t="399260" d="4479" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This was the story of one of the last
great Kurdish dynasties to rule at the</s></p>
<p t="403780" d="1740" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">eastern gate of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="410240" d="1879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The historian Vladimir Minorsky called this age,</s></p>
<p t="412160" d="4439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">when Daylamite and Kurdish
dynasties rose to power,</s></p>
<p t="416640" d="719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Iranian Intermezzo.</s></p>
<p t="417400" d="4360" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Together with the Marwanids, the
Shaddadids and the Hasanwayhids,</s></p>
<p t="421800" d="3919" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Annazids were a strong link in the political
presence of the Kurds in the Middle Ages.</s></p>
<p t="425760" d="4399" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Today, the Kurds living around Sarpol-e Zahab,</s></p>
<p t="430200" d="2820" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Shahrazur and Daquq are the heirs
of this thousand-year-old story.</s></p>
<p t="433540" d="4039" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">One tribe, one pass, an emirate
of one hundred and thirty years.</s></p>
<p t="437620" d="4119" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The pages of Kurdish history that
others tried to erase, here on Dirok.</s></p>
<p t="441780" d="4280" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">If you enjoyed this video, don't forget
to subscribe and share your thoughts in</s></p>
<p t="446100" d="1019" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the comments.</s></p>
</body>
</timedtext>
